        {
          "id": "the_activity_chain_reproduces_the_worked_examples",
          "description": "docs/process-brakewear.md 6.1-6.2, at the four named stages a wrong factor shows up at. The activity half is shared by all three pollutants -- brake wear is weighted by the same source-hours-operating as running-exhaust energy -- so an error here moves all 375 rows.\n\nRE-ADDRESSED FOR THE ONE-DAY RUN, BY KEY (docs/esm-conventions.md §40, §42). `<day key=\"5\">` was an out-of-range 0-based INDEX into the sorted DayOfAnyWeek list [2, 5]: it selected nothing and MOVES fell back to BOTH day types, so this fixture ran a weekend it never meant to (moves.rs PR #55). Every day-crossed axis halved -- `activity_rows` 82 to 41, `output_rows` 750 to 375, `runspecday_rows` 2 to 1 -- with the day OUTERMOST, so each coordinate was looked up by the KEY it named and re-read at that key's new ordinal, and every weekend claim was replaced by the SAME COHORT on the day the run selects. travelFraction normalised over the whole HPMS group is 1.609175e-03 for the oldest cohort; over source type 21 alone it is 4.10e-03 and every row is 2.55x high. averageSpeed is the ARITHMETIC mean. The four `sho` values are the snapshot's own, to its six stored significant figures.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
          "tolerance": {
            "rel": 2e-06
          },
          "assertions": [
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.act_travelFraction",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 0.01804420293236,
              "coords": {
                "activity_rows": 1
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.act_travelFraction",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 0.001609174853309,
              "coords": {
                "activity_rows": 41
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.act_averageSpeed",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 56.361205525,
              "coords": {
                "activity_rows": 1
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.act_sho",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 260.057,
              "coords": {
                "activity_rows": 1
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.act_sho",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 83.6255,
              "coords": {
                "activity_rows": 21
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.act_sho",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 23.1918,
              "coords": {
                "activity_rows": 41
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "the_collapsed_base_rate_reproduces_both_of_the_references_generator_tables",
          "description": "`rtDay_meanBaseRate` IS `baserate_1_2020.meanBaseRate` on the 9101 rows and `baserate_9_2020.meanBaseRate` on the 11609 rows -- the reference's own S14 intermediates, computed here. This is the most direct check of W available, and the brakewear half is the check this fixture adds: the base rate factorises exactly around W, the two rate paths share the same 46 weights, and the two rate tables are seven orders of magnitude apart -- 333,651 kJ per source-hour against 0.0600 g -- so a wrong W is a wrong number in every one of these cells and no shared scale factor can hide behind it.\n\nSIXTEEN ASSERTIONS BECAME THIRTEEN AT THE DAY CORRECTION (docs/esm-conventions.md §42). Each rate row used to be asserted at BOTH day types and is now asserted at the one the run selects; the weekday numbers are the reference's own and were mostly already here, and the four that were only ever asserted at the weekend -- rate rows 41 and 88 on 9101, 369 and 416 on 11609 -- were re-read out of `baserate_1_2020` and `baserate_9_2020` at hourDayID 95 rather than rescaled from the weekend ones.\n\n`rtDay_meanBaseRateACAdj` is the INCREMENTAL A/C load of sbweighted.rs:459. It reaches emissionQuant multiplied by an A/C factor this document computes to be exactly 0, so the OUTPUT cannot see it -- but the generator tables carry it in their own column, so it is checked here anyway, on both paths: 26,899.6 for 9101 and an exact 0 for 11609, which is what an absent `fullacadjustment` row has to produce.\n\nThe tolerance is the reference's six-significant-figure column storage.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
          "tolerance": {
            "rel": 2e-05
          },
          "assertions": [
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.rtDay_meanBaseRate",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 176680.0,
              "coords": {
                "rate_rows": 1,
